{"codes":["P0301"],"vehicle":"2015 Ford F-150 XLT","engine":"5.0L V8","fuel_type":"Gasoline","drive":"4WD","transmission":"6-Speed Automatic","supercharged":false,"turbocharged":false,"hybrid":false,"electric":false,"performance_tier":"sport","mpg_city":"","mpg_highway":"","mpg_combined":"","tank_capacity":"","horsepower":"385","rpm":734,"speed":0,"coolant_temp":201,"safety_level":"STOP","safety_meaning":"High Urgency - Immediate Attention Required","safety_description":"Continuing to drive could cause permanent damage to your engine, transmission, or other major components. This could turn a $500 repair into a $5,000+ repair.","safety_action":"Go to a mechanic immediately or have vehicle towed","dont_panic":"Your 2015 Ford F-150 XLT is showing code P0301. This code means that the engine is detecting a misfire on Cylinder 1. Simply put, it means that cylinder is not firing correctly, which is like a hiccup in the engine's breathing. The vehicle's own readings showed that the total trim for Bank 1 at idle was 5.4% and the total trim for Bank 2 at idle was 4.3%. The misfire was recorded when the engine was running hard, specifically at 68% engine load and with the coolant temperature at 197°F. This is something that needs attention because the misfire happened while the engine was working hard and at operating temperature.","likely_causes":"1. The ignition coil (the part that creates the spark) for Cylinder 1 is suspected because the misfire is confined to one cylinder (P0301), which points at a component specific to that cylinder rather than anything shared by the whole engine. This is supported by the evidence that the misfire was recorded at 2814 RPM and 68% engine load with coolant at 197°F.","symptoms":"1. Rough idling or stumbling acceleration - This happens because the engine is not getting the power it needs from one cylinder.\n2. Loss of power - The engine won't feel as strong as normal because one cylinder is misfiring.\n3. Check Engine Light flashing - A flashing light indicates a severe misfire that needs immediate attention.\n4. Engine running unevenly - You might feel the vehicle shaking or vibrating slightly while driving.","if_ignored":"If you ignore this, the unburned fuel from the misfire can reach the catalytic converter (a device that cleans up exhaust gases), causing it to overheat and potentially get destroyed. In the next few days or weeks, the misfire could worsen, and the catalytic converter could fail. Long-term, this turns a simple ignition repair into a much more expensive converter replacement. There are no specific safety concerns mentioned, but the engine running poorly can affect overall vehicle performance.","quick_checks":"Swap the ignition coil for P0301 with a neighbouring cylinder and rescan — if the code follows the coil, the coil is the fault\nInspect that cylinder's spark plug\nCompare fuel injector operation against the other cylinders\nRescan after a few days of normal driving to see whether they confirm","diy_fix":"This repair is NOT recommended for DIY because diagnosing the exact cause (coil, plug, or injector) requires specialized diagnostic equipment and knowledge to ensure the correct component is replaced. A professional mechanic should handle this.","urgency":"Go to a mechanic today - don't drive more than necessary.","repair_cost":"Parts: $400 - $1,200\nLabor: $300 - $800\nTotal at independent shop: $700 - $2,000\nTotal at dealer: $1,000 - $3,000 (dealers charge more)\nThis repair requires an experienced mechanic familiar with modern engine diagnostics.","known_issues":"Ford Motor Company (Ford) is recalling certain 2015-2019 Ford F-150 and 2017-2019 Ford F-250 Super Duty, F-350 Super Duty, F-450 Super Duty, and F-550 Super Duty pick-up trucks equipped with an engine block heater. Water and contaminants may get into the block heater cable's splice connector causing corrosion and damage.\nFord Motor Company (Ford) is recalling certain 2015-2019 Ford F-150 and 2017-2019 Ford F-250 Super Duty, F-350 Super Duty, F-450 Super Duty, and F-550 Super Duty pick-up trucks equipped with an engine block heater and previously remedied under recall 18V894. During the previous repair, the block heater cable's splice connector may have been damaged.\nFord Motor Company (Ford) is recalling certain 2015-2018 Ford F-150 Regular Cab and SuperCrew Cab vehicles. If a front seat belt pretensioner deploys as the result of a crash, the sparks may ignite materials such as carpeting or insulation within the B-pillar area.\nFord Motor Company (Ford) is recalling certain 2015-2017 Ford F-150, and 2017 F-250, F-350, F-450, and F-550 Super Duty trucks. The doors on these vehicles may have a bent or kinked actuation cable or the door latches may freeze after water gets into the latch. Either condition can result in the doors opening while driving although they appear to be closed.\nFord Motor Company (Ford) is recalling certain 2015-2017 F-150, and 2017 F-250 and F-350 trucks equipped with a crew cab. The left rear inflatable seat belt buckle assembly may be inadequately attached to its mounting bracket. As such, these vehicles fail to comply with the requirements of Federal Motor Vehicle Safety Standard (FMVSS) number 210, \"Seat Belt Assembly Anchorages.\"","owner_reports":"The 5.0L V8 is generally more reliable long-term (Source: ClearDrive local KB).","data_sources":["NHTSA Recalls Database","ClearDrive Known Issues KB"],"obd_source":"Synthetic fixture: f150-2015-p0301-coil","trim":"XLT","vehicleImageURL":"/image-proxy?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffile.kelleybluebookimages.com%2Fkbb%2Fbase%2Fevox%2FCP%2F10213%2F2015-Ford-F150%2520Super%2520Cab-front_10213_032_1837x807_YZ_cropped.png","mileage":96500,"pending_codes":["P0316"],"permanent_codes":[],"capability_limitations":[],"code_definitions":[{"code":"P0301","description":"Cylinder 1 Misfire Detected","tier":"standardized_unverified","source":"ml/data/sae_j2012.json (not yet verified against SAE J2012)","system":"powertrain","subsystem":"ignition_misfire","caveat":"Standardized code definition. Our copy of the standard table has not yet been verified against the SAE J2012 document."}],"differential":[{"rule":"misfire_triage","conclusion":"The misfire is confined to one cylinder (P0301), which points at a component specific to that cylinder rather than anything shared by the whole engine.","confidence":"high","basis":"heuristic","next_checks":["Swap the ignition coil for P0301 with a neighbouring cylinder and rescan — if the code follows the coil, the coil is the fault","Inspect that cylinder's spark plug","Compare fuel injector operation against the other cylinders"],"evidence":[{"pointer":"freeze_frames[P0301]","restatement":"the misfire was recorded at 2814 RPM and 68% engine load with coolant at 197°F"}]}],"not_assessed":[],"is_synthetic":true,"code_status":[{"rule":"code_status_pending","conclusion":"There are pending codes (P0316) — faults the computer has seen once but not yet confirmed. They are an early warning, not a confirmed failure."}],"recommended_checks":["Swap the ignition coil for P0301 with a neighbouring cylinder and rescan — if the code follows the coil, the coil is the fault","Inspect that cylinder's spark plug","Compare fuel injector operation against the other cylinders","Rescan after a few days of normal driving to see whether they confirm"],"safety":{"verdict":"stop_driving","basis":"heuristic","reasons":[{"rule":"misfire_active","statement":"An active misfire code (P0301) is stored. Unburned fuel from a misfire reaches the catalytic converter, so this needs attention before it becomes a converter repair (heuristic).","raises_to":"caution","evidence":[{"pointer":"dtc_codes","restatement":"codes present: P0301"}]},{"rule":"misfire_under_load","statement":"The misfire occurred while the engine was working hard or fully warm. Raw fuel entering a hot catalytic converter overheats and destroys it, which turns an ignition repair into a converter replacement (heuristic).",